<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Ch. XV: eggs as solids of revolution of a definite, species-varying family of ovoid meridian curves (Preston 1953 family on Biggins et al. 2018 / Deeming 2018 data) (Aves egg shells: Uria aalge (Common Guillemot), Vanellus vanellus (Northern Lapwing), Strix aluco (Tawny Owl), Podiceps cristatus (Great Crested Grebe); 30 auk + penguin species (1389 eggs); 181 species / 30 orders)",
        "Thompson (1917) Ch. XV: eggs as solids of revolution of a definite, species-varying family of ovoid meridian curves (Preston 1953 family on Biggins et al. 2018 / Deeming 2018 data) faithfully reproduces Aves egg shells: Uria aalge (Common Guillemot), Vanellus vanellus (Northern Lapwing), Strix aluco (Tawny Owl), Podiceps cristatus (Great Crested Grebe); 30 auk + penguin species (1389 eggs); 181 species / 30 orders morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-034-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-034-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-034-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-034-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-034-5> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) thompson/claims/t1917-034_on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-certain-other-hollo.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Aves egg shells: Uria aalge (Common Guillemot), Vanellus vanellus (Northern Lapwing), Strix aluco (Tawny Owl), Podiceps cristatus (Great Crested Grebe); 30 auk + penguin species (1389 eggs); 181 species / 30 orders specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-034_on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-certain-other-hollo.py> ;
    schema:about "Aves egg shells: Uria aalge (Common Guillemot), Vanellus vanellus (Northern Lapwing), Strix aluco (Tawny Owl), Podiceps cristatus (Great Crested Grebe); 30 auk + penguin species (1389 eggs); 181 species / 30 orders" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Anything about eggshell microstructure, colour, or non-avian hard-shelled eggs beyond Thompson's mention.",
        "does_not_claim: That oviduct pressure is demonstrated to be the cause of the family; only the geometric claim and the internal consistency of Thompson's equation are tested.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Computer-vision segmentation and Preston weighted least squares (Biggins Analysis.R estimator) on 12 eggs in 5 photographs; axisymmetry residual; Cartesian-oval and ellipsoid controls.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Preston (1953) four-parameter meridian family with Biggins et al. (2018) indices, closed-form polar asymmetry R_pole = p(+/-1)^2 and exact volume functional (Lean Laws 1-2).",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Thompson closure: pole / equator curvature constraints + equator position determine the Preston coefficients; mean-curvature (excess pressure) profile of every egg; pressure-asymmetry path sphere -> ellipsoid -> conical.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: eta^2 / Kruskal-Wallis across 30 species and 15 orders, KS test, RMSE / breadth, Spearman asymmetry vs relative egg mass.",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson 1917 p. 652: eggs are simple solids of revolution; forms range from spherical (owl, penguin, tortoise) through ellipsoidal (grebe, cormorant, pelican) and ovoid (hen) to blunt-pointed conical (guillemot, puffin, sandpiper, plover, curlew); prolate never oblate.",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson 1917 p. 654 footnote: Steiner's Cartesian oval r + m r' = c 'fails in such a case as the guillemot'.",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson 1917 p. 655 (5): the most unsymmetrical / tapered eggs are large relative to the parent bird (apteryx the exception, p. 656).",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson 1917 pp. 657-659: cone-ended-cylinder balance p cos^2(theta) = p' cos^2(theta'); envelope equation p_n + T(1/r + 1/r') = P; spherical egg <=> uniform p_n.",
        "transfer_boundary: Biggins et al. (2018) eggs are auks and penguins (plus a 10-egg range set); order-level breadth comes only from Deeming's outline PCs; museum photographs are oblique-lit and bound the axisymmetry test from above.",
        "transfer_boundary: The Preston family is a 1953 descriptor adopted as the quantitative reading of Thompson's 'definite family'; Thompson gave no algebraic family.",
        "transfer_boundary: The Thompson closure is a kinematic reading of his equilibrium equation (curvature <-> excess pressure), not a dynamic oviduct simulation; friction and tangential forces he mentions are not modelled." ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-034_on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-certain-other-hollo.py; output: docs/media/t1917_034_on_the_shapes_of_eggs_and_of_c_comparison.png; organism: Aves egg shells: Uria aalge (Common Guillemot), Vanellus vanellus (Northern Lapwing), Strix aluco (Tawny Owl), Podiceps cristatus (Great Crested Grebe); 30 auk + penguin species (1389 eggs); 181 species / 30 orders; environment: python 3.12.3, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, Pillow 13.0.0, scikit-image 0.26.0, lean 4.35.0; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Thompson 1917 p. 652: eggs are simple solids of revolution; forms range from spherical (owl, penguin, tortoise) through ellipsoidal (grebe, cormorant, pelican) and ovoid (hen) to blunt-pointed conical (guillemot, puffin, sandpiper, plover, curlew); prolate never oblate. | Thompson 1917 p. 654 footnote: Steiner's Cartesian oval r + m r' = c 'fails in such a case as the guillemot'. | Thompson 1917 p. 655 (5): the most unsymmetrical / tapered eggs are large relative to the parent bird (apteryx the exception, p. 656). | Thompson 1917 pp. 657-659: cone-ended-cylinder balance p cos^2(theta) = p' cos^2(theta'); envelope equation p_n + T(1/r + 1/r') = P; spherical egg <=> uniform p_n.; reconstructed_in_script: Preston (1953) four-parameter meridian family with Biggins et al. (2018) indices, closed-form polar asymmetry R_pole = p(+/-1)^2 and exact volume functional (Lean Laws 1-2). | Thompson closure: pole / equator curvature constraints + equator position determine the Preston coefficients; mean-curvature (excess pressure) profile of every egg; pressure-asymmetry path sphere -> ellipsoid -> conical. | Computer-vision segmentation and Preston weighted least squares (Biggins Analysis.R estimator) on 12 eggs in 5 photographs; axisymmetry residual; Cartesian-oval and ellipsoid controls. | eta^2 / Kruskal-Wallis across 30 species and 15 orders, KS test, RMSE / breadth, Spearman asymmetry vs relative egg mass.; transfer_boundary: The Preston family is a 1953 descriptor adopted as the quantitative reading of Thompson's 'definite family'; Thompson gave no algebraic family. | Biggins et al. (2018) eggs are auks and penguins (plus a 10-egg range set); order-level breadth comes only from Deeming's outline PCs; museum photographs are oblique-lit and bound the axisymmetry test from above. | The Thompson closure is a kinematic reading of his equilibrium equation (curvature <-> excess pressure), not a dynamic oviduct simulation; friction and tangential forces he mentions are not modelled.; does_not_claim: That oviduct pressure is demonstrated to be the cause of the family; only the geometric claim and the internal consistency of Thompson's equation are tested. | Anything about eggshell microstructure, colour, or non-avian hard-shelled eggs beyond Thompson's mention.]; other sources: On Growth and Form (Ch. XV, On the Shapes of Eggs, and of Certain Other Hollow Structures, pp. 652-669) <https://archive.org/details/ongrowthform1917thom>" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.4412>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1038/078111a0>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1111/jav.01528>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaj1945>,
        <https://doi.org/10.2307/4081145>,
        <https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.8kv2b20>,
        <https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.q8rp3> ;
    pk:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-code-traceability>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-model-fidelity>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-morphometric-validation>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-overall>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-parameter-expressivity>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-repro-package>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-source-traceability>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-034-specimen-match> ;
    pk:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-10>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-11>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-12>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-13>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-14>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-15>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-16>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-17>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-5>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-6>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-7>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-8>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/2026-10-28-t1917-034-on-the-shapes-of-eggs-and-of-c-audit-v2-thompson1917-t1917-034-9> ;
    pk:hypothesisType "Geometric: Preston (1953) four-parameter ovoid family of meridian curves for egg solids of revolution, closed by Thompson's (1917) envelope equation p_n + T(1/r + 1/r') = P" ;
